.TH TAM 1 "October 2026" "Tamlinux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tam \- terminal entry point, offline guide, and system inspector for Tamlinux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tam
.br
.B tam welcome
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam show
.RI [ PAGE | \- ]
.RI [ --level \ LEVEL ]
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam search
.RI [ --json ]
.IR TERM ...
.br
.B tam explain
.RI [ --level \ LEVEL ]
.RI [ TOPIC ]
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam explain topics
.RI [ --pipe ]
.RI [ --terse | --verbose ]
.RI [ --level \ LEVEL ]
.br
.B tam man
.RI [ SECTION ]
.IR PAGE
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam man
.RI [ --help ]
.br
.B tam info
.RI [ MANUAL \ [ NODE ]]
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam tldr
.RI [ COMMAND \ [ SUBCOMMAND... ]]
.RI [ --platform \ OS ]
.RI [ --list ]
.RI [ --search \ KEYWORD ]
.RI [ --pipe ]
.br
.B tam inspect
.RI [ --json | --check \ [ ID ]]
.RI [ --terse ]
.br
.B tam check
.RI [ ID ]
.RI [ --json | --terse ]
.br
.B tam install
.RI [ --help ]
.br
.B tam ask
.RI [ --json ]
.RI [ --model \ MODEL ]
.RI [ --server \ URL ]
.IR QUESTION
.br
.B tam upgrade
.RI [ --check | --apply ]
.RI [ --channel \ STAGE ]
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B tam switch
.IR STAGE
.RI [ --version \ X.Y.Z \ --corpus-version \ A.B.C ]
.br
.B tam switch --rollback
.br
.BR "tam \-\-help" " | " "tam help"
.br
.B tam \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tam
is the offline terminal entry point, documentation browser, and health inspection command for Tamlinux workstations. It starts in under 5 milliseconds, consumes negligible memory (<4 MB RSS), makes zero network calls by default, and runs without background polling daemons.
.PP
.B tam
renders the bundled semantic HTML knowledge corpus through a built-in ANSI terminal pager or as clean plain text when redirected or invoked with
.BR \-\-pipe .
It operates identically on minimal recovery systems, legacy hardware, and modern graphical desktops.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B tam
Print a concise introduction to the command and exit.
.TP
.BR "tam welcome" " | " "tam hello" " | " "tam hi" " [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]"
Open the first-run Tamlinux welcome orientation. When displayed successfully to completion in an interactive terminal, atomically records state in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/tamlinux/welcome_seen
to prevent repeated display on subsequent logins.
.TP
.B tam show \fR[\fIPAGE\fR|\fB\-\fR] [\fB\-\-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR] [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]
Open the offline user guide in the built-in interactive pager, or stream plain text. Directly view a specific page or index. If
.I PAGE
is omitted, opens the root User Guide index (
.I index.html
). If
.I PAGE
is
.BR topics ,
displays the interactive topic catalog.
.I PAGE
may be a bare guide path, a
.BI page: PATH
address (e.g.
.BR page:shell/pipelines.html ),
or an address beginning with
.BR man: ,
.BR info: ,
.BR tldr: ,
or
.BR tam: .
Manual page addresses contain parentheses (such as
.BR 'man:ls(1)' )
and must be quoted in the shell. If
.B \-
is specified, reads semantic HTML from standard input and displays it in the pager. Supports depth filtering via
.BI \-\-level \ LEVEL
or
.BI ?level= LEVEL
(where
.I LEVEL
is
.BR all " (" 0 ),
.BR beginner " (" 1 ),
.BR intermediate " (" 2 ),
or
.BR expert " (" 3 )).
.TP
.B tam search \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] \fITERM...\fR
Search the bundled offline corpus index using field-weighted ranking (topic slugs, titles, keywords, and summaries). Supports multi-term prefix queries and bounded fuzzy suggestions ("Did you mean?"). If no guide page matches, suggests searching system manual pages via
.BR "tam man" .
The Guide reader also provides an in-reader search prompt through the
.B tam:search
route; results link directly to matching guide pages. This keeps the CLI and JSON output contract unchanged.
.TP
.B tam explain \fR[\fB\-\-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR] [\fITOPIC\fR] [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]
Display a topic guide to concepts and keywords in the Tamlinux guide.
.B tam explain
always displays a topic guide listing relevant pages with explanations, never jumping directly to a single page (use
.B tam show
to directly view a page). Bare
.B tam explain
presents a dynamically generated catalog of all topic keywords from page metadata in the built-in pager. Input containing shell characters (pipes, redirections, subshells) is treated purely as data and is never executed. Multi-word queries fall back to ranked index search.
.TP
.B tam explain topics \fR[\fB\-\-pipe\fR] [\fB\-\-terse\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
Display the complete catalog of curated explanation topics. Interactively opens a hyperlinked table in the built-in pager, allowing immediate selection and navigation. Under
.BR \-\-pipe ,
emits clean plain text. Supports
.B \-\-terse
(one topic slug per line) and
.B \-\-verbose
(exhaustive metadata including paths, levels, and keyword arrays).
.TP
.B tam man \fR[\fISECTION\fR] \fIPAGE\fR [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]
Display a hyperlinked manual page. Bare
.B tam man
opens a discovery landing page with actions to open a page, find by keyword, or browse a section. Keyword and section results use the installed manual index with bounded output and no shell command construction; page-name input is treated as data. Selecting a manual result opens it in the same reader. Noninteractive bare invocation prints the landing page without prompting, and
.B tam man --help
continues to print usage.
.PP
System roff manual pages are converted to semantic HTML and displayed in the built-in pager. Cross-references (e.g.
.BR ls (1))
and section headings are automatically turned into active hyperlinks that can be followed with
.B Tab
and
.BR Enter .
.TP
.B tam info \fR[\fIMANUAL\fR [\fINODE\fR]] [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]
Browse system-wide GNU Texinfo reference manuals with hyperlinked menus, cross-references, and node navigation. Bare
.B tam info
opens the top-level Info directory catalog (\fI(dir)Top\fR), categorizing all installed manuals on the host. Specifying
.I MANUAL
opens that manual's \fITop\fR node; specifying
.I NODE
navigates directly to that specific section (e.g. \fBtam info coreutils "head invocation"\fR). In interactive mode, dedicates keys \fBn\fR (next node), \fBp\fR (previous node), \fBu\fR (parent node), \fBt\fR (top node), and \fBd\fR (directory catalog).
.TP
.B tam tldr \fR[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fISUBCOMMAND...\fR]] [\fB\-\-platform\fR \fIOS\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-search\fR \fIKEYWORD\fR] [\fB\-\-pipe\fR]
Browse or display concise, example-first command cheat sheets from the curated offline
.B tldr-pages
corpus. Bare
.B tam tldr
opens the discovery and index page (\fBtam:tldr\fR) with actions to open a page by name, find pages by keyword, or browse the A\(enZ and platform directory index. Multi-word command names (such as \fBtam tldr git commit\fR) normalize automatically to hyphenated page names (\fBgit-commit\fR). Option placeholders (\fI{{[-s|--long]}}\fR) render both short and long forms (\fB[-s|--long]\fR), and cross-references in \fBSee also:\fR lines, alias notices, subcommand \fBtldr\fR invocations, and companion \fBman:\fR pages are rendered as live hyperlinks.
.TP
.B tam inspect \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR|\fB\-\-check\fR [\fIID\fR]] [\fB\-\-terse\fR]
Inspect host state, kernel version, hardware architecture, mount points, DRM graphics devices, package managers, and privilege escalation. The operating system row names the installed distribution from os-release; the Tamlinux cultivar row shows the cultivar name and version deployed for the invoking user (under \fBpkexec\fR, the user who ran it). With
.BR \-\-check ,
evaluates system health findings according to the offline Check Contract.
.TP
.B tam check \fR[\fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR|\fB\-\-terse\fR]
Run system progression and workstation health checks according to the offline Check Contract.
.TP
.B tam install \fR[\fB\-\-help\fR]
Tamlinux installation framework overview. Component installation commands will be available in future releases.
.TP
.B tam ask \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-model\fR \fIMODEL\fR] [\fB\-\-server\fR \fIURL\fR] \fIQUESTION\fR
Ask a grounded question answered strictly from the offline knowledge corpus via a local LLM inference server (e.g. Ollama). Strictly offline by default; requires explicit interactive consent to download models; never runs background daemons; mandates source citations.
.TP
.B tam upgrade \fR[\fB\-\-check|\-\-apply\fR] [\fB\-\-channel\fR \fISTAGE\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
Checks Development, Test, Run and Release source metadata from the fixed public tam repository. Checks require curl and bounded HTTPS requests; ordinary help, version reporting and guide browsing remain offline.
.B \-\-apply
builds and activates a forward update after an interactive preview and confirmation. It requires Git, curl, a C compiler, Make, pkg-config, Python 3, zlib, ncursesw and D-Bus development files. It builds selected upstream sources as the current user under
.IR ~/.local/dev/opt/tam/ .
.TP
.B tam switch \fISTAGE\fR
Selects Development, Test, Run or Release source, including an explicitly confirmed downgrade. Release can be pinned with both
.B \-\-version
and
.BR \-\-corpus-version .
Source commands require an interactive terminal and never install operating system packages.
.TP
.B tam switch \-\-rollback
Restores the previous locally recorded generation or the packaged command without network access. A retained recovery launcher is printed after activation for use when an older selected tam has no upgrade commands.
.TP
.BR "tam \-\-help" ", " "tam help"
Print a concise synopsis of commands and options.
.TP
.B tam \-\-version
Print version information for the
.B tam
command binary, the bundled offline
.B knowledge corpus
(and its build revision),
.BR libtam ,
and the installed Tamlinux cultivar metadata.
.SH PAGER KEYBOARD CONTROLS
When viewing pages with
.BR "tam show" ,
.BR "tam explain" ,
.BR "tam man" ,
or
.BR "tam info" ,
the interactive pager provides the following controls:
.P
The letter keys sit on the home row. Each hand has left, up, down and right
under one finger each, mirrored: the index fingers point inward, the middle
fingers move down, the ring fingers move up and the little fingers point
outward.
.TP
.BR d ", " k ", " Down
Move the attention line down one line. From the breadcrumb row, return to the attention line where it was.
.TP
.BR s ", " l ", " Up
Move the attention line up one line. At the first document line, focus the last breadcrumb link; while the breadcrumb row is focused, move to the previous breadcrumb link.
.TP
.BR a ", " j ", " f ", " ;
Move the attention column left
.RB ( a ", " j )
or right
.RB ( f ", " ; )
from link to link along the attention line, wrapping at either end. The
column is a band as wide as the caught link, drawn down the whole screen; the
link where it crosses the attention line is the selected link. Moving the
attention line or following a link clears it. On the breadcrumb row these keys
move between breadcrumb links. A line without links has nothing to catch.
.TP
.BR Space ", " PgDn
Scroll down by one full screen.
.TP
.BR b ", " PgUp
Scroll up by one full screen.
.TP
.B x\fR, period (\fB.\fR), \fBHome
Jump to the top of the document. The fingers that move up one line reach one
row down to go all the way up.
.TP
.B c\fR, comma (\fB,\fR), \fBEnd
Jump to the bottom of the document. The fingers that move down one line reach
one row down to go all the way down.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift-Tab
Select the next or previous hyperlink anywhere on the page, in reading order,
including the breadcrumb links. If no hyperlink has been selected yet, the
first hyperlink visible on the screen is selected.
.TP
.BR g ", " Enter ", " Right
Follow the selected hyperlink. When no link is selected,
.B Right
advances forward along previously visited history, restoring the saved viewport and line position.
.TP
.BR n ", " p
In GNU Info manuals, navigate to the Next or Previous sibling node. When search is active,
.BR n / N
cycles through search matches instead.
.TP
.B Left
Always go back in reader history, restoring the saved viewport, cursor and link focus.
.TP
.B u
Navigate up to the parent node in an Info manual. On ordinary pages, go back in reader history.
.TP
.B t
Jump directly to the \fITop\fR node of the current Info manual.
.TP
.B i
Jump directly to the root Info directory catalog (\fI(dir)Top\fR).
.TP
.B ` \fR(backtick)\fP
Return immediately to the root User Guide index (
.I index.html
) from any depth, manual page, or Info node.
.TP
.B /
Search for text in the current document. Press
.B n
for the next match,
.B N
for the previous match, or
.B Esc
to cancel.
.TP
.B =
Toggle address display in the status row (\fBPath =\fR \fIaddress\fR).
Guide pages show \fBpage:\fR\fIpath\fR, while \fBman:\fR, \fBinfo:\fR,
\fBtldr:\fR, and \fBtam:\fR addresses appear unchanged. Standard input shows
\fBPath = (standard input has no address)\fR. Any displayed address can be
passed to \fBtam show\fR to reopen the page. Dismiss with \fBEsc\fR, a second
\fB=\fR, or by moving to another page.
.TP
.BR 0 ", " 1 ", " 2 ", " 3
Switch the reading depth of the current guide page or topic view:
.B 0
for all levels,
.B 1
for beginner,
.B 2
for intermediate, and
.B 3
for expert.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Exit the pager.
.TP
.BR h ", " ?
Open navigation help. Press
.BR h ", " ?
or
.B Esc
to close it without changing the page, focus or history.
.SH TERMINAL SUPPORT
The interactive pager reads terminal capabilities through terminfo and updates
only rows whose contents changed. It reflows pages after a terminal resize,
preserving the reading position, link selection and search state. The Linux
virtual console and monochrome terminals use reverse video for the focused
line; terminals without usable styling continue to support navigation.
.PP
When standard output is not a usable terminal, the command emits plain text
without terminal control sequences. Set
.B NO_COLOR
to disable color while retaining the interactive pager.
.SH OUTPUT MODIFIERS
.TP
.BR \-\-pipe ", " \-p
Emit clean, unescaped plain text suitable for piping into other Unix tools (e.g.
.BR grep ,
.BR head ).
Appends a numbered
.B Links:
appendix for all hyperlinks in the document.
.TP
.B \-\-terse
Output compact, machine-readable text (e.g. topic slugs only, or task ID status pairs).
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Output detailed diagnostics, keyword arrays, and prerequisites.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit structured JSON data (supported by
.BR search ,
.BR inspect ,
.BR check ,
and
.BR ask ).
.TP
.BI \-\-level \ LEVEL
Filter content or topic listings to
.BR all " (" 0 ),
.BR beginner " (" 1 ),
.BR intermediate " (" 2 ),
or
.BR expert " (" 3 ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TAM_KNOWLEDGE_DIR
Overrides the compiled knowledge corpus directory. Used for development builds and test suites.
.TP
.B TAM_DATADIR
Compiled fallback directory for the knowledge corpus.
.TP
.B TAM_ASK_MODEL
Default local model name for
.B tam ask
(default:
.BR llama3.2:1b ).
.TP
.B TAM_ASK_SERVER
URL of the local LLM inference server (default:
.BR http://127.0.0.1:11434 ).
.TP
.B TAM_ASK_ALLOW_FETCH
Set to
.B false
to prevent interactive model download prompts.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
User data directory (defaults to
.I ~/.local/share
). Checked for
.IR tamlinux/knowledge .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_DIRS
System data directories (defaults to
.I /usr/local/share:/usr/share
). Checked in order for
.IR tamlinux/knowledge .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
User state directory (defaults to
.I ~/.local/state
). Checked for
.IR tamlinux/welcome_seen .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tamlinux/welcome_seen
Timestamp recording the completion of
.BR "tam welcome" .
.TP
.I <datadir>/index.html
The root User Guide entry point and overarching Table of Contents.
.TP
.I <datadir>/index.tsv
Build-time corpus index with topic names, paths, titles, summaries, levels, and keywords.
.TP
.I <datadir>/index.json
JSON projection of the corpus index.
